`for … else` in an imperative body. The else case is "the body never ran",
which passForElse states as a flag set by the body's first statement and
tested after the loop -- three statements every backend already emits, so no
language grows a case for the construct.

Before that pass the generic per-language statement path read a loop's head
and body and nothing else, and this else block was silently dropped from the
generated code in all three languages while the interpreter honoured it.

This fixture is why the harness exists. Its predecessor asserted the flag with
`grep -count=1 '__ran0 = true'`, and that count is satisfied wherever in the
body the assignment lands. Moving it from the body's first statement to its
last broke `break` and `continue` on every compiled target and passed the
entire suite. Read the golden below and the position is the first thing on the
page; a count cannot see it.

Two loops in one body get a flag each rather than sharing one, which is
likewise a fact about the whole body rather than about any one line of it.

deny * `__ran0 = true;?\n\s*\}` -- the flag is the body's first statement, never its last: a `continue` must not be able to skip it

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    var xs list[int] = []
    var note = ""
    var total = 0

    button(text="run", @click {
        for var x = xs {
            if x == 0 {
                continue
            }
            total = total + x
        } else {
            note = "empty"
        }
        for var y = xs {
            total = total + y
        } else {
            note = "still empty"
        }
    })
    text(value=note)
}

window {
    main
}
